{"about":"6bdc16fc61c220036acbccfe16be6e8f7747318e53c86d376b81b4161576af73","body":"The objection relies on the assumption that 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) functions as a proxy for probability, but Popper developed verisimilitude specifically to escape the probabilistic framework. Verisimilitude is a logical relation concerning content and truth, not a measure of likelihood derived from repeated observations. To claim it is a 'covert proxy' ignores Popper's rigorous attempt to define truth-content and falsity-content logically, independent of any inductive uniformity principle regarding nature's structure.\n[would settle: Demonstration that verisimilitude can be defined purely logically without invoking the principle of the uniformity of nature.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}